Subject: [PATCH 4/5] winealsa: usable MIDI - port names, subscribable ports, rawmidi output

Three related fixes for real MIDI hardware.

1. Port naming. A class-compliant USB device names its sequencer client after
   the product ("DDJ-400") and its port after the client ("DDJ-400 MIDI 1"), so
   "client - port" yields "DDJ-400 - DDJ-400 MIDI 1" where Windows reports
   plain "DDJ-400". Applications match hardware on that string, so the
   decoration makes a supported controller unrecognisable.

2. Enumerate only SUBSCRIBABLE ports. The only thing this driver does with a
   port is snd_seq_connect_from/to, which needs CAP_SUBS_READ/CAP_SUBS_WRITE;
   CAP_READ/CAP_WRITE alone is not enough. PipeWire registers ports that are
   readable but not subscribable, and offering them as MIDI devices puts
   unusable entries ahead of the real hardware in a list applications walk by
   index.

3. Send to hardware via rawmidi, not the sequencer. The sequencer delivers a
   userspace SysEx to a rawmidi port in 32-byte chunks, so a 66-byte message
   reaches the USB MIDI packetiser as 32 + 32 + 2; that packetiser emits whole
   3-byte continuation packets, sends 63 bytes and holds one back, and whether
   the last packet joins the same URB is a race. A device that receives a SysEx
   split across two USB transfers can hang: a Pioneer DDJ-400 stops accepting
   output permanently and needs a physical power cycle. Windows delivers a
   midiOutLongMsg SysEx as one transfer, and so do native Linux MIDI
   applications, because they write rawmidi.

   Destinations with no card behind them keep the sequencer path, so
   application-to-application MIDI is unchanged. Where rawmidi is used the
   sequencer port is deliberately not subscribed, so short and long messages
   cannot travel by two routes and be reordered. The write result is checked;
   the sequencer path discarded it and returned MMSYSERR_NOERROR regardless.

Measured on a DDJ-400 with rekordbox 7.2.17: 0 successful device handshakes in
12 runs before, 4 of 4 after, with no hangs.

